				"paragraphStyle" : {
					"__type" : "textStyle"
				},
				"string" : "The Vault maintains two views of configuration data, #config (the raw input form) and #options (the structured ready to use form).\n\nTheVault performs a dual role, \n\n1) from the client end and 2) from the host/environment end.\n## 1) From the Client\nA vault is empty by default and can be filled in.\n\na) A client class needs to be up and running easily\n\n`csClient := CloudsmithClient new.`\n\nAdding an api key ought to be as simple as.\n\n`csClient vaultConfig at: 'CLOUDSMITH_API_KEY' put: 'csa_xyz'`\n\n## 2) From the Host Environment\nThe TheVault current is populated by the world of env files and osEnvironment\ntaking into consideration obfuscation of passwords, and multiple client configurations\nfor multiple apps and hosts.\n\nThe whole kitchen sink is in there.\nvault := TheVault current\n\nWhen the vault is added to a client, the client gets an efficient copy, focusing on its own data.\n\nb) A fully configured client may be created by a dedicated factory class, and in the process\n\n`TheFactory newCloudsmithClient`\n```.\ncsClient>>setVault: aVault\n\t^ vault :=  aVault copyForApp: self appId) \n\t\t\t\t\ncsClient vaultOptions\n(#default #sandbox) \n```\n\n\n\n\t\t\nok\n\t\t\nThe Client chooses what form of the vault that it keeps, in this case\nall the data that is not cs specific is discarded, but the \n\n\nWe have a tiered dictionary datastructure implemented as a linked list populated from a number of sources, {{gtClass:TTDictionaryTiered|show=comment|view}}  \n\n{{gtClass:TTVault}} Holds a master copy this data in its \n\n\n\n"
			},
			{
